  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 211 AM EDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nIn the upper levels, a ridge is over the south and Midwest today and \ntonight. A trough is over the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic. The \nnorthwest flow pattern continues with storms forming upstream in the \nMidwest then riding the ridge down through Kentucky and into \nSouthwest Virgina and Northeast Tennessee. This afternoon and early \nevening showers and storms will be isolated to scattered in \ncoverage. CAMs did not do well Thursday with placement and coverage \nof storms. CAMs are split for this afternoon whether coverage will \nbe isolated or scattered. Some guidance suggests a stronger ridge \nthis afternoon weakening later in the evening. Therefore, I cannot \nrule out another storm cluster moving through Southwest Virginia and \nNortheast Tennessee late in the evening like the past two nights. If \nthat happens, I think the intensity and coverage will be like the \npast two nights and not a full blown MCS. SPC has Southwest Virginia \nand Northeast Tennessee in a marginal risk for severe today and \ntonight which seems appropriate. Flash flooding will continue to be \na concern in places that have received a lot of rain recently. At \nthe moment confidence is not high enough for a flood watch but one \nmay be issued later for Southwest Virginia and Northeast Tennessee \nif confidence in heavy rainfall increases.\n\nHeat indices will be high again today especially in the Southern \nTennessee Valley where locations like Chattanooga may briefly reach \nheat advisory criteria of 105 degrees heat index. There is better \nconfidence in needing a heat advisory for most of the Tennessee \nValley on Saturday and Sunday. \n\nThe ridge over the south will continue through this weekend bringing \nisolated to scattered showers and storms mainly in the afternoon and \nearly evening hours. We may still get some late night northwest flow \nstorms in Southwest Virginia and Northeast Tennessee. SPC has that \narea outlined in a marginal risk for that possibility. Isolated \nflooding issues will still be a concern this weekend in places that \nhave received a lot of rain.\n\nBy early next week, the ridge weakens and the trough dips into the \nTennessee Valley. By mid week, a ridge will move back over the \nTennessee Valley. NBM has the best chances of rain on Monday mainly \nin the northeast half of the region. Otherwise, POPs look typical \nfor this time of year with mostly diurnal convection.",
